|~ `::keyword`, the kind of keywords, its constructor and its
   refusal, and the comparisons of keywords. Each resides on
   `::qlang/keyword`, the kind whose module declares it, and compares
   two keywords by their names in the one order. ~|

::keyword
  |~~ The kind of a keyword, `:k`, a name that stands for itself. It is
      apart from the string so that no name is read by its look: every
      key of a map is a keyword, `/k` reads a field by it, and a keyword
      names a binding of the scope; `keyword` makes one of a string and
      a string of one. A bare keyword is a name of letters of any script,
      digits, `_` and `-`; a slash joins names into a path, `:domain/user`;
      any other text is written in quotes, `:"foo bar"`, and the quotes
      around a bare name change nothing. ::link~(::map | doc) ::link~(::binding | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :values)

      ~(:k | type | eq ::keyword)
      ~(::keyword:k | eq :k)
      The three forms, and the quoted form of a bare name:
      ~([:данные :domain/user :"$ref"] * keyword | eq ["данные" "domain/user" "$ref"])
      ~(:"foo" | eq :foo)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/keyword}

::KeywordPayloadNotKeywordError
  |~~ `::keyword` reads its payload as the keyword it is; a string
      becomes one by `keyword`.
      ~(
        ::keyword"k"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::KeywordPayloadNotKeywordError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

:gt
  |~~ Whether the subject's name follows the name of the keyword the
      call gives.

      ~(:b | gt :a | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::keyword | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/gt})

:gte
  |~~ Whether the subject's name follows or equals the name of the
      keyword the call gives.

      ~(:b | gte :b | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::keyword | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/gte})

:lt
  |~~ Whether the subject's name precedes the name of the keyword the
      call gives.

      ~(:a | lt :b | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::keyword | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/lt})

:lte
  |~~ Whether the subject's name precedes or equals the name of the
      keyword the call gives.

      ~(:a | lte :a | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::keyword | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/lte})

:keyword
  |~~ The name of the keyword, as a string: the reverse of `keyword` on
      a string, so `"foo" | keyword | keyword` answers `"foo"`.

      ~(:foo | keyword | eq "foo")
      ~(:"foo bar" | keyword | eq "foo bar")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::string | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/keyword})

:runLaws
  |~~ The results of the laws of the page of the binding the keyword
      names in its scope, one `{:law :says :actual :ok :error}` per law,
      `:says` the caption of the claim the law proves. A keyword after
      it runs the laws of the member the binding holds under it.

      ~(:limit |~~ Examples: ~(true) ~~| 10 | :limit | runLaws * /ok | eq [true])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:anchor #[::keyword ::null] | :returns ::vec | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/runLaws})
